● Aliasing in Python

  Aliasing is when two or more variables refer to the same object in memory. Changing one affects the other.

  Basic example

  a = [1, 2, 3]
  b = a          # b is an alias — same object, not a copy

  b.append(4)
  print(a)       # [1, 2, 3, 4] — a changed too!
  print(a is b)  # True — same object

  This does NOT happen with immutable types

  x = 10
  y = x
  y = 20
  print(x)       # 10 — unchanged, because int is immutable

  Immutable types (int, float, str, tuple) can't be modified in place, so aliasing is harmless.

  Common aliasing traps

  1. Function arguments
  def add_item(items):
      items.append("new")  # modifies the original list!

  my_list = ["a", "b"]
  add_item(my_list)
  print(my_list)  # ["a", "b", "new"]

  2. Nested lists
  row = [0, 0, 0]
  grid = [row, row, row]  # three aliases to the same list

  grid[0][1] = 5
  print(grid)  # [[0, 5, 0], [0, 5, 0], [0, 5, 0]] — all rows changed!

  3. Default mutable arguments
  def add_item(item, items=[]):  # BAD — shared across all calls
      items.append(item)
      return items

  print(add_item("a"))  # ["a"]
  print(add_item("b"))  # ["a", "b"] — unexpected!

  Fix:
  def add_item(item, items=None):
      if items is None:
          items = []
      items.append(item)
      return items

  How to avoid aliasing — make copies

  a = [1, 2, 3]

  # Shallow copy (top-level only)
  b = a.copy()
  b = a[:]
  b = list(a)

  # Deep copy (nested objects too)
  import copy
  c = copy.deepcopy(a)

  Dictionary aliasing — same rules

  info = {"ip": "10.0.0.1", "mac": "aa:bb:cc"}
  backup = info          # alias!
  backup["ip"] = "none"
  print(info["ip"])      # "none" — original changed

  # Fix: copy
  backup = info.copy()

  Quick rule of thumb

  ┌────────────────────────┬──────────┬────────────────┐
  │          Type          │ Mutable? │ Aliasing risk? │
  ├────────────────────────┼──────────┼────────────────┤
  │ int, float, str, tuple │ No       │ Safe           │
  ├────────────────────────┼──────────┼────────────────┤
  │ list, dict, set        │ Yes      │ Be careful     │
  ├────────────────────────┼──────────┼────────────────┤
  │ Custom objects         │ Yes      │ Be careful     │
  └────────────────────────┴──────────┴────────────────┘

  The key insight: assignment (=) never copies data — it just creates another name pointing to the same object. Use
  .copy() or copy.deepcopy() when you need independence.
